# Synopsis

Read a file, or list the entries of a directory. File content is
returned with 1-based line numbers; directory entries are listed one
per line. Reading is read-only and needs no approval.

# Returns

The result is a header line followed by the requested lines or
entries.

For a file, each line is prefixed with its 1-based line number:

    regular file: src/main.c, lines 1-3 of 42
    1: #include <stdio.h>
    2:
    3: int main(void) {

The header reports the range of lines shown and the total number of
lines. When the last line of the file lacks a trailing newline, the
header adds "no trailing newline"; when the offset is past the end,
the header reports "offset N is beyond the end (M lines)" instead of
content.

For a directory, each entry is listed by name, with a marker for its
type: a regular file is shown by name alone, a directory ends in "/",
a symbolic link shows its target after " -> ", and a special file
shows its type in parentheses:

    directory: src, entries 1-5 of 5
    lib/
    main.c
    Makefile
    link -> lib/main.c
    pipe (FIFO)

Special types are FIFO, socket, character device and block device,
shown as "(FIFO)", "(socket)", "(character device)" and "(block
device)".

# Notes

- offset (default 1) is the first line or entry to read, limit
  (default 1500) the maximum number to read. Read a further page by
  raising offset.
- A symbolic link is reported as its target and is not followed:
  "symbolic link: <path> -> <target>". Device, FIFO and socket paths
  are reported by type with no content.
- A file containing a NUL byte is treated as binary and its content is
  not shown; an empty file or directory is reported as "empty"; a path
  that does not exist is reported as "no such file or directory".
- A pathname, and each entry name in a directory listing, is shown in
  shell-quoted form when it contains characters outside
  [A-Za-z0-9_./-].
- Use the reported line numbers to refer to specific lines when
  editing.
